  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern this morning is defined by a strong\nupper-low moving through the Pacific Northwest, a strong ridge \nof high pressure off the coast of British Columbia Canada, another\ntrough along 150W in the Eastern Pacific poised to undercut the\nupper-high, and a generally zonal flow across the Desert Southwest.\n\nA weak disturbance in the westerly flow aloft will track across\nSoutheast Arizona today, providing breezy westerly winds to our\nneck of the woods. This disturbance appears to be weaker than \nprogged 24-hours ago, so the resulting winds should be a hair \nless than expected (20-foot west winds 10 to 20 mph with gusts \nto 30 mph). There is a dry airmass setup across the area, with \nminimum RHs between 10-20% anticipated today. The combination of\nthese elements should still provide an elevated fire weather \nconcern today, but confidence is low-medium on any locations\nactually getting up to critical levels. \n\nThe southern periphery of the upper-low over the Pacific Northwest\nsags south and drags across Southeast Arizona Thursday. This\nwill provide stronger winds aloft to mix their way down to the \nsurface. Given the breezy to locally windy westerly winds \npoised to impact our neck of the woods Thursday (20-foot west \nwinds 15 to 20 mph with gusts 30-35 mph) and the continued dry \nairmass across the region, we can expect to see elevated to near\ncritical fire weather conditions Thursday. This is supported by\nthe 21/21Z Short Range Ensemble Forecast (SREF) probability \nbetween 20-40 percent for reaching critical fire weather \nconditions (RH<=15% and 20-foot wind speed=>20 mph) along the \nArizona/New Mexico border 24/00Z Thursday afternoon. \n\nThe upper-low in the Eastern Pacific eventually undercuts the\nupper-high center Thursday afternoon, and rapidly makes its way\ninto California by 25/12Z Saturday morning. This feature will\namplify the flow as it moves into the Desert Southwest. Stronger\nwinds in the mid-levels will provide Southeast Arizona with breezy\nto windy southwest to west winds this weekend. Lower-level \nmoisture begins to push into the lower deserts west of Tucson \nSaturday and into the eastern valleys Sunday. This may end up\nproviding yet another day of near critical fire weather conditions\nSaturday before subsiding Sunday. \n\nThere is decent agreement with the ensemble members of the GEFS\nand ECMWF replacing another upper-low under the ridge Sunday in\nthe wake of the one that will impact us. It then strengthens/ \namplifies this trough and imports deeper subtropical moisture \ntoward Arizona the middle of next week. If this moisture makes \nits way into the area, it would be capable of supporting showers/\nthunderstorms. However, my confidence is medium at best that we\nwill see this plume advect into Southeast Arizona. Pattern \nrecognition tells me that it would be more likely get shunted \neast of our forecast area. Time will tell.",
